# Eight, wrap up

The ideal car is a typical case of a "good balance sheet plus poor trend". The hundreds of billions of cash provide a very large security cushion, which allows companies to stay in business and allows for some downside protection in the current valuation ($11.87/ADS). But only a lack of security cushions — the retreat of incremental dividends, the fall of profit-breaker cliffs, the loss of core teams, and the overall increase in competition — are not yet at the bottom. The market performance of the L series and pure power i8/i9 in the second half of 2026 will determine whether the ideal is a "reflection after a temporary downfall" or a "start of a structural recession". Until the point of entry is confirmed, this is a target of risk gains relative to asymmetries — upbound space depends on a highly uncertain transition success, with downside risks that may continue to deteriorate.

